ZIP(1)                         P32W Unix                          ZIP(1)

NAME
       zip - make or update a zip archive

SYNOPSIS
       zip [-rq0..9] archive[.zip] file ...

DESCRIPTION
       zip adds the files to a zip (PKZIP) archive, making the
       archive if there is none. An entry already in the archive with
       the same name is replaced; the other entries are kept as they
       were. The archives are exchangeable with the Mac's zip and
       unzip and with Finder.

       If archive does not end in .zip, .zip is added to the name
       unless a file (not a directory) of that name exists: so
       zip -r src src, with src a directory, makes src.zip.

       Entries are compressed with deflate, unless -0 is given or the
       file is empty; then they are stored. A directory named on the
       command line becomes a directory entry (name/); with -r,
       everything below it is added too. Other kinds of file
       (devices, FIFOs) are skipped with a message, and the exit
       status is 1.

       Entry names are the paths as given, less any leading / and
       ./, of files and directories alike: zip -r a /usr/ken stores
       usr/ken/ and usr/ken/x. The archive itself (old or new) is
       not added to itself, under whatever name it is met.

       Each entry records its Unix permission bits and file type,
       its owner and group (the Info-ZIP "ux" extra field) and its
       modification time (the "UT" field, which Info-ZIP reads, and
       the older "UX" field, which macOS's ditto and Finder read), as
       well as the DOS date and time, which is local time by TZ.

       The new archive is written beside the old one as zinnnnn.tmp
       (nnnnn the process id) and renamed over it when it is
       complete, so an interrupted zip leaves the old archive intact.
       The new archive keeps the old one's permission bits; a new
       archive is made mode 0666 less the umask. If the archive
       would have no entries at all, none is made.

       For each file added, zip prints "adding:" with the name and
       the method with the percentage saved.

OPTIONS
       -r     Recurse into directories.

       -q     Quiet: do not print the "adding:" lines.

       -0     Store the files without compression.

       -1 .. -9
              Compression level, from fastest (-1) to best (-9);
              the default is -6.

       Options may be combined (-rq9) and must come before the
       archive name.

EXAMPLES
       zip notes notes.txt todo           make or update notes.zip
       cd /usr; zip -r /mnt/ken.zip ken   a whole home directory
       zip -r9q src.zip src               best compression, quietly
       zip src.zip src/cmd/ls.c           replace one entry in src.zip

EXIT STATUS
       0 if all went well (even if nothing was added; zip then says
       "nothing added"); 1 if a file could not be read or added, or
       was not a regular file or directory; 2 for a
       usage error, or if the old archive is not a zip archive or is
       damaged, or the new one cannot be written.

SEE ALSO
       unzip(1), tar(1), gzip(1), kermit(1), deflate(3), crc32(3)

BUGS
       There is no -d (delete), -u or -f (update, freshen), -m
       (move), -j (junk paths), -x or -i (exclude, include), -l,
       -@, -z (comment), -e (encryption), -y and no -T.

       Without -r a directory gives only its own entry, not its files.

       Entry names are limited to 255 characters. There is no Zip64:
       an archive and its entries must stay under 4 GiB. An existing
       archive whose comment is longer than about 1 KiB is not
       recognized.

       Entries kept from an old archive are copied with their data
       as it was, but their extra fields are written anew: the time
       from the "UT" or "UX" field (else from the DOS time) and the
       owner from the "ux" or "UX" field; an entry that recorded no
       owner still records none. Other extra fields (and comments)
       of old entries are dropped.
